    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nElroy Air Scores a $70M Deal for Its Autonomous Heavy-Cargo Drone in the UAE\n10 photos\nPhoto: Elroy Air\nElroy Air, the name behind one of the most innovative and advanced autonomous, hybrid-electric cargo VTOLs (vertical takeoff and landing) in the US, is taking new steps to increase its presence in the UAE, one of the biggest and most promising markets for AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) overall.\nUS-based technology company Elroy Air is gearing up to end the year on a high note, with one of its most significant purchase deals so far. This new agreement with the Abu Dhabi-based Barq Group is big not just because of the actual monetary value (a total of $70 million, starting with a $10.25 million initial deposit), but also because it opens the door to an extended presence in the MENA space (Middle East and North Africa) market.\nIn fact, this purchase agreement between Elroy Air and Barq Group is only a part of a much more ambitious, large-scale plan. The two have previously confirmed plans for a joint venture that will support a manufacturing and service facility in Abu Dhabi, specifically dedicated to producing and distributing the Chaparral cargo drone in the entire MENA region, which would translate to a huge step up for Elroy Air.\nIn the meantime, the Chaparral VTOL drones agreed upon through this recent collaboration will be manufactured in the US. More specifically, Elroy’s exclusive US manufacturer, Kratos Defense & Security Solutions, will start rolling out these drones at its facility in Sacramento, California. Production is set to begin here before the end of this year, with first deliveries to Barq Group in Abu Dhabi scheduled for the beginning of next year.\nThe contract specifies five firm orders plus 15 production slot reservations, with the condition that Barq Group will make pre-delivery payments for all the reservations. According to Elroy, the initial deposit will help fund half of the price of each of the five Chaparral drones included in the firm order.\nThe Chaparral VTOL drone brings together multiple advantages, from the hybrid-electric propulsion and vertical landing ability to a fully automated cargo loading/unloading process. The pod at the center of its unique cargo system needs to be only pre-loaded by personnel, after which the VTOL picks it up, delivers, and unloads it at the destination without any human intervention.\nThe vertical takeoff and landing capability allows the Chaparral to operate independently of any conventional runways or airstrips, and its electric propulsion supported by a hybrid system is also not dependent on dedicated charging infrastructure. An extended range of over 300 miles (483 km) is coupled with a heavy-payload cargo of up to 300 lbs (136 kg).\nChaparral is the only autonomous, hybrid-electric heavy-cargo drone that was selected to participate in the eIPP , America’s pioneering electric aviation and AAM initiative.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChaparral Elroy Air autonomous cargo VTOL hybrid-electric VTOL AAM cargo drone\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT How Long Would It Take to Travel a Light-Day by Car, Train, Or Airplane?",
